{"chunk_id": "61caad77373d", "text": "The passage argues that kingship should follow virtue and fitness rather than mere birth order. Examples are given: King Yayati bypassed his elder sons and crowned the obedient Puru, establishing that respectful conduct toward elders can outweigh primogeniture. A similar story follows with King Pratipa\u2019s sons\u2014Devapi, though virtuous and beloved, is barred from the throne because of a skin disease, since a physically defective king was considered inauspicious. Devapi withdraws to the forest, Vahlika leaves for another kingdom, and the youngest, Santanu, becomes king. This precedent is used to justify why Dhritarashtra, being blind, did not rule and why Pandu\u2019s son Yudhishthira is the rightful heir. Despite repeated counsel from elders and Krishna\u2019s diplomatic efforts\u2014including the proposal of granting the Pandavas merely five villages\u2014Duryodhana rejects all advice and marches to war, setting the stage for Kurukshetra.\nKrishna recounts Dhritarashtra\u2019s court rejecting peace; Duryodhana marches to war despite elders\u2019 counsel, while lineage precedents justify Yudhishthira\u2019s rightful claim.\nThemes: rightful kingship, ignored counsel, fate and war, dynastic precedent, diplomacy failure", "vector": [0.005378890782594681, -0.0026877103373408318, -0.01585211232304573, 0.0007101003429852426, -0.008328605443239212, 0.0404214970767498, -0.013887949287891388, -0.01270806323736906, -0.02337561920285225, -0.01029276754707098, 0.01646287553012371, -0.015588372945785522, 0.014047580771148205, 0.01890593394637108, -0.027526039630174637, 0.010112314485013485, 0.055524032562971115, -0.01729573681950569, 0.04272574558854103, -0.004216356202960014, -0.011882143095135689, -0.010022087953984737, -0.002668623812496662, -0.0010020353365689516, -0.020071938633918762, 0.033009037375450134, 0.011902964673936367, -0.018600551411509514, 0.0010931293945759535, -0.017379023134708405, -0.007370815612375736, 0.0090642